SVELTE_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="your-project-url"
SVELTE_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="your-publishable-or-anon-key"

# Both values reach the BROWSER -- `$env/static/public` is inlined into the
# client bundle -- so the URL has to be one the browser can resolve, whether the
# app is served by `bun run dev` or by the container. Against a Supabase CLI
# stack on this machine that is http://127.0.0.1:54321 in either case; take the
# key from `supabase status`. `host.docker.internal` is a name that exists only
# inside a container, so a browser on the host cannot resolve it (Linux in
# particular) and every client-side auth and data request fails.
#
# Docker: Compose passes these as BUILD ARGS, because Vite inlines them at build
# time. Editing them and restarting does nothing -- rebuild with
# `docker compose up --build`.
#
# WHICH FILE: Compose interpolates `${...}` from the shell or from `./.env`, and
# from NOWHERE else -- `.env.local`, which is where `bun run dev` reads these
# from, is invisible to it. Unset variables are not an error: Compose warns and
# substitutes an empty string, so the image builds green with an empty Supabase
# URL baked in and fails only in the browser. Put all four values in `./.env`
# (gitignored) before building, or point Compose at another file explicitly:
#
#   docker compose --env-file .env.local up --build

# Server-side only (`$env/dynamic/private`), read per request by
# /api/ai/generate. Unlike the pair above, these do take effect on a restart.
# Without the token the AI proxy calls the Railway backend with no Bearer header
# and rewrites come back 401. The URL is optional; the route falls back to the
# production backend.
RAILWAY_API_TOKEN=""
# RAILWAY_API_URL="https://your-backend.up.railway.app"
